PLACEMENT LAW (why these points):
- service_start sits BETWEEN constructor success and start_composed_runtime:
  before it, a seeds refusal cannot yet exist; after it, the thread is
  guaranteed alive across EVERY recoverable refusal (mfa_activate_failed,
  MRS_ERR_CONVERSION/CONTEXT/START from prepare, and the ASYNC init-thread
  refusal surfaced after module_start returned 0 — see the companion diff
  runtime_owner-insession2-refused.diff).
- settle_startup AFTER start_composed_runtime returns: dispatch is held for
  the whole wiring window; the settle happens whether the wiring succeeded
  or refused (both are "the outcome is known").
- note_initializer_refused at each acknowledged failure branch gives the
  boot an observable refused-mode triage line and records the flag the
  host-test directive names ("works when the initializer refused flag is
  set"). The ASYNC refusal (init thread, post-return) is covered by the
  companion diff.
- service_stop at the top of module_stop: the poll thread is joined before
  the runtime owner stop and LONG before probe_lifecycle_stop closes the
  diagnostic channel, so nothing dispatches mid-teardown and no effect can
  outlive the channel.

